    <sys:String x:Key="Setup.Experimental.JvmHost.Title">Jvm.NET lifecycle host</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Setup.Experimental.JvmHost.Enable">Use the Jvm.NET host to control the Minecraft lifecycle</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Setup.Experimental.JvmHost.Summary">Minecraft runs inside an isolated PCL N host process. Third-party authentication no longer loads authlib-injector, and custom offline skins use the same session bridge to reach the game.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Setup.Experimental.Benefits.Title">Benefits</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Setup.Experimental.Risks.Title">Risks and limitations</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Setup.Experimental.JvmHost.Benefits">• Records JVM initialization, main-class invocation, Minecraft bootstrap, and window subsystem lifecycle nodes.&#10;• Third-party authentication does not load an extra authlib-injector JAR into the game.&#10;• Offline profiles can use local or borrowed skins in-game.&#10;• JVM crashes stay isolated in the host process instead of taking down the launcher.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Setup.Experimental.JvmHost.Risks">• Embedding the JVM and transforming authlib bytecode can conflict with some Java builds, mods, or authentication servers.&#10;• Adds a small amount of startup time and memory usage.&#10;• PCL N verifies and transforms third-party texture signatures, so behavior is not identical to authlib-injector.&#10;• A host or loopback-bridge failure aborts that game launch.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Setup.Experimental.JvmHost.Scope">The authentication and skin bridge listens only on 127.0.0.1 and ends with the game host. Disabling this option immediately restores the normal Java process and authlib-injector path.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Setup.Experimental.JvmHost.Confirm.Title">Enable the Jvm.NET lifecycle host</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Setup.Experimental.JvmHost.Confirm.Message">Benefits: richer JVM and Minecraft lifecycle logs; no authlib-injector for third-party authentication; local offline skins work in-game; a host crash does not take down the launcher.&#10;&#10;Risks: this feature embeds a JVM in an isolated process and transforms authlib bytecode. It can conflict with some Java builds, mods, or authentication servers; it adds some startup time and memory; authentication and skins pass through a 127.0.0.1 bridge for this game session; PCL N verifies and then transforms third-party texture signatures, which is not identical to authlib-injector. Disable the option to restore the traditional launch path if anything fails.&#10;&#10;Each host runs one JVM and exits with the game. Do you understand the risks and want to continue?</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Setup.Experimental.JvmHost.Confirm.Enable">Understand risks and enable</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Setup.Experimental.AiRepair.Title">Minecraft AI error repair</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Setup.Experimental.AiRepair.Enable">Analyze Minecraft crashes with a local model or OpenAI-compatible API and assist repair</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Setup.Experimental.AiRepair.Summary">The conventional analyzer still locates every crash first. The model can report live stages, create an auditable chained repair plan, and act only through safe operations allowed by the error handler.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Setup.Experimental.AiRepair.Benefits">• Requests redacted environment, instance, crash, and launch details only when needed.&#10;• Supports repair plans with up to four dependent steps.&#10;• Local inference prefers the GPU and supports custom GGUF models.&#10;• Optionally uses an OpenAI-compatible API and thinking models.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Setup.Experimental.AiRepair.Risks">• Local first use downloads an approximately 491 MB model and 10–37 MB runtime.&#10;• Inference commonly uses an additional 0.8–1.5 GB of memory.&#10;• Models can be wrong; generated text does not guarantee a successful repair.&#10;• Online mode sends explicitly requested, redacted diagnostics to the provider you configure.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Setup.Experimental.AiRepair.Scope">The model cannot run commands, read arbitrary files, or bypass the repair allowlist. Read-only context is category-scoped, redacted, and length-limited. Account names, UUIDs, tokens, passwords, API keys, full local paths, and server addresses are never provided. Raw thinking is neither displayed nor logged.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Setup.Experimental.AiRepair.Confirm.Title">Enable Minecraft AI error repair</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Setup.Experimental.AiRepair.Confirm.Message">Benefits: the conventional analyzer still locates each crash first. A model can write a readable explanation and propose a chained plan of up to four operations from the error handler's allowlist. Local mode prefers the GPU, and you may instead connect an OpenAI-compatible API.&#10;&#10;Risks: the default Gemma 4 E2B model initially downloads approximately 3.11 GB, Gemma 4 E4B approximately 4.98 GB, plus a 10–37 MB runtime. Larger models need more memory and inference time. Models can be wrong. Online mode sends only the model-requested, redacted, length-limited runtime environment, instance metadata, crash reports, logs, launch method, or login method to your configured provider. Account names, UUIDs, tokens, passwords, API keys, full local paths, and server addresses are never sent.&#10;&#10;The model cannot execute commands, read arbitrary files, or bypass the repair allowlist. Raw thinking is neither shown nor logged; only stages, progress, and auditable rationale are retained. Do you understand the download, performance, privacy, and misclassification risks and want to continue?</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Setup.Experimental.AiRepair.Confirm.Enable">Understand risks and enable</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Setup.Experimental.AiRepair.Provider.Title">Inference provider</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Setup.Experimental.AiRepair.LocalModel.Title">Local model</sys:String>
